   "fsPath": "Implementation/QubitCount",
   "files": 20,
   "decls": 412,
   "summary": "<p>How many qubits the lowered order-finding circuit needs at once, with an exact formula. We walk the lowered circuit in order and keep the set of live qubits: a gate makes the qubits it touches live, extending a register brings its reserve in, and deallocating releases it. <code>AddScaled</code> and <code>Negate</code> also borrow a few clean scratch qubits while they run. The peak is the largest the live set ever gets.</p><p>This is not <code>LowGate.qubitCount</code>, the number of qubits the circuit ever touches: the allocator gives every child of a phase-product recursion its own reserve, but the children run one after another, so only one is live at a time. <code>Policy.orderFinding_peak_formula</code> proves the peak is the base registers plus the most expensive step, for any admissible policy, and the same for the balanced circuits of <code>Reference/Balanced</code>.</p><p>At 2048 bits, with the standard \\(k = 2\\) table and a 4096-qubit exponent register, the peak is 19,319 for the plain circuit and 13,449 for the balanced one (17,291 under the policy option <code>negation := .scratch</code>), checked by the kernel (<code>decide +kernel</code>) in <code>Proofs/Numerals.lean</code> and <code>Proofs/BalancedNumerals.lean</code>. The folder reads <code>Statement &lt; Proofs &lt; Main</code>.</p>",
